# Semantic search

Semantic search orders a corpus against a query and hands back a prefix. It is
the **read path of AI memory** over a [[hql/knowledge|knowledge]] base.

```hql
import lexical

cards
| lexical("bearer token authorization")
| take(5)
| expand(depth = 1)
| Graph
```

A retrieval is written out because it is an extension capability rather than the
core's business — see [[hql/extensions]].

It has a vocabulary of its own but mints no node type and no edge type, so it is
not a peer domain in the sense [[type-system]] uses: nothing in it is anyone's
parent.

## Vocabulary

```hql
type Hit<T> {
    value     : T
    score     : Float
    query     : String
    retrieval : Data
}

type Ranking<T> : Seq<Hit<T>> {
    hits      : List<Hit<T>>
    query     : String
    retrieval : Data
}
```

`retrieval` is the record of how the hits were produced, an open tree with
the keys `query`, `index`, `model`, `revision`, `metric` and `approximate`.
The declarations are `std/retrieval.hql`.

`Ranking` narrows `Seq` the way a `Relation` narrows an `Edge`: it adds what
makes the order meaningful — the query the order is relative to, and the rule
that broke ties. A bare `Seq<Hit<T>>` is an ordered pile of scored things with
no statement about where the order came from. See [[orderable]] for the tie rule.

The parent is applied: `Seq<Hit<T>>` is built from the parameter rather than
receiving it, and the type system declares it that way. So a ranking passes
anywhere a `Seq<Hit<T>>` or a `Collection<Hit<T>>` is asked for, and its
element is `Hit<T>`. `take` and `filter` keep a ranking a ranking, because a
concrete sequence keeps its type through a prefix and a filter. `of_type` and
`map`, which change the element, make it a `Seq` of the new element, since a
ranking's argument is the ranked value and not its element. `sort` by another
key makes it a `Seq<Hit<T>>`, since the new order is not the retrieval's. A
ranking's `hits` field holds its elements as a `List`. A ranking reports the element type it was built from:
`lexical` and `semantic` build a `Ranking<Card>`, and a hit's `value` is what it
retrieved, here the card.

## A score is evidence, never relevance

The knowledge domain rests on one rule: interpretation is a step, and the rule
used must be retained with the result. A similarity score is an interpretation —
produced by a named model over a particular chunking under a particular metric
against an index at a particular revision.

```text
Proposition   Relevant(card, query)
Evidence      the Hit — score, model, index revision, metric
Provenance    which index answered, and when
```

A card scoring 0.91 is not more true than one scoring 0.42; it is more similar
to a query under one model. That is why **`retrieval` is not optional on
`Hit`**: a hit that cannot say how it was produced is not evidence.

## An index contributes embeddings, never scores

| Belongs in `card.metadata` | Cannot | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Embeddings, chunk identities | | a property of the card |
| Index membership, indexed revision | | a property of the card |
| Staleness against the card's content | | a property of the card |
| | A score | exists only relative to a query |
| | A rank | exists only relative to a query and a corpus |

A card has an embedding whether or not anyone ever searches; it has no score
until someone asks something. A retrieval extension contributes under a key
named after itself — the matcher writes `metadata.lexical` — because two
retrievals cannot both be "the" search, and a key either of them could claim is
one they would take turns overwriting. See [[fields]] and [[card-type]].

## Cards are not concepts

`semantic` ranks **representations**. AI memory wants to recall **knowledge**.

```hql
semantic(q) : Set<Card>  -> Ranking<Card>      // over representations
recall(q)   : Knowledge  -> Ranking<Concept>   // over knowledge
```

A `Concept` can exist with no card at all — imported, derived, or read from a
store — which is why `Graph<Concept, Relation>` is parameterized by `Concept`.
Those cardless concepts have no text, so text search cannot reach them, and a
memory that silently cannot recall part of itself is worse than one that says
so. `recall` is the AI-memory operation and is not implemented.

**A `RelationCard` hit is not a seed.** It contributes an edge and is not a
node, so treating every hit as a node reintroduces the duplication the card
split prevents — see [[card-type]].

## Approximation is semantics, not optimization

Approximate nearest-neighbour search does not preserve observable values, so
pushing `semantic | take` into a store is illegal under the [[pipes]] optimizer
rule. The resolution is not to weaken that rule: **`semantic` declares itself
approximate in its own contract**, so the difference is part of what the step
means rather than a liberty the executor took. `approximate` is a key of every
hit's `retrieval` for the same reason — a consumer can see which answer it got.

Purity follows the same line. The same query against a changed index gives
different answers; pinning an index makes `semantic` reproducible, and without
pinning it is an effect. What is pinned today is the model's revision, carried
in every hit's `retrieval`, and a card whose text has drifted from its vector says so
rather than scoring quietly.

## What is built

Two retrievals, each an extension and each honestly named. [[lexical]] matches
shared spellings with `hql.hashbag.v1`, a hashed bag of words compared by
cosine, offline, deterministic and exact, reporting `approximate: false`. It
models no meaning and its name no longer claims to.

[[semantic]] scores against vectors a language model computed ahead of time,
stored in an SQLite index a vault names in `hql.toml`. The model stays out of
this binary, so the index carries the queries too and a query it does not hold
is a failure naming the command that would add one. Scoring is exact brute force
over stored vectors, so `approximate` is `false` here as well; an approximate
index is step four and is not built.

Replacing one retrieval with another changes the numbers, not the shape — which
is the point of `retrieval` travelling on every `Hit`, and why it carries the
model's `revision` as well as its name.

`expand` and `Graph` stay apart. `expand(depth, direction)` traverses at least
one step and produces a graph carrying why each node is present; `Graph(xs)`
projects a collection without traversing, and projecting a graph yields it
unchanged.

## Open

- **Chunking.** A card is not a retrieval unit; a passage is.
- **Multiple indexes**, hybrid retrieval, and whether a fused score can still
  carry an honest `retrieval`.
- **Filtering before or after** — `cards | of_type(ConceptCard) | semantic(q)`
  versus filtering the ranking.
- **The write path** — remembering, reconciliation, consolidation of
  co-retrieval into derived relations, and eviction. A score may *propose* that
  two concepts are the same and may never *decide* it.

See the [semantic-search requirements](../../models/requirements/semantic-search.md) for the
staged implementation path and the full set of open questions.
